00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:03.750 align:start position:0%
in january the long beach city council  passed an  ordinance requiring a four-month four  dollar an hour  pay boost for employees of large grocery  store chains  they called it hero pay for essential  workers  often overlooked during the pandemic  when we looked at it  it was very clear that grocery workers  were interacting with  more people daily than probably any  other profession  and they were being incredibly  overworked and they deserved that  additional support  they weren't expecting the backlash the  state's grocery association  filed suit to block the extra pay and  kroger one of the us's biggest  supermarket chains  abruptly announced it would close two  long beach stores  leaving 200 employees jobs in doubt eric  cortez has worked for kroger for 14  years  he showed us a letter sent to employees  this  advise you of our company's decision to  close and permanently terminate  the entirety of the operations of  business and economic reasons  including the economic costs mandated by  the long beach ordinance  what shocked employees and politicians  is that grocery chains made  enormous profits last year coveted 19  restrictions on restaurants meant  more people bought food to cook at home  big grocery chains  kroger in particular are rolling in  money  kroger is a real standout so kroger in  the first three quarters  of 2020 when you look at their net  earnings their total profits  they have doubled their profit compared  to 2019 so they earned an additional 1.3  billion dollars in profits  that's an astonishing growth and if you  figured kroger would  share some of that bonanza with its  workers guess again  by and large the companies have not  passed these windfall profits on to  their workers who  is important to a note that grocery and  retail workers typically earn very low  wages  cortes and his co-workers are  disappointed and  angry well i just think that they're  being greedy the california grocers  association says  its members have had to buy protective  equipment or ppe  they have not considered all of the  expenses that we have paid  in ppe long beach mayor robert garcia  says it's simply a matter of fairness  they deserve these few extra bucks  just for a few months to help them and  their families but workers  may get the last word more and more  california cities have approved  or are in the process of approving hero  pay  including los angeles oakland irvine and  san jose rob reynolds al jazeera long  beach  california
 

